<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Severia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Seversk land, Siveria, Sivershchyna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Severia was a historical region in the basin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desna-river">Desna</a> and Seim rivers, now divided between north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, named after the East Slavic Severians. Its towns included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novhorod-siverskyi">Novgorod-Seversky</a>, Starodub, Putivl and Rylsk. Held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> from the fourteenth century, it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in the war of 1500–1503, was ceded to Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> in 1618 and returned to Russia in the mid-seventeenth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/severia.jpg" alt="Severia" width="320" height="305" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stefan Maria Kuczyński · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ziemie_Czernihowsko-Siewierskie_w_XV_wieku.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
